    "turns": [
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Why does Kernel PCA deserve its own place in the history of artificial intelligence?",
            "sourceIds": [],
            "claimIds": [],
            "contentOrigin": "editorial-hook"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Bernhard Scholkopf, Alexander Smola, and Klaus-Robert Muller showed that PCA could be kernelized by solving an eigenvalue problem in an implicit feature space. Kernel PCA made nonlinear structure accessible through pairwise similarities.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-nonlinear-component-analysis-as-a-kernel-eigenvalue-problem"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.historical-background"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "A kernel matrix replaces explicit coordinates, letting PCA find nonlinear components.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-nonlinear-component-analysis-as-a-kernel-eigenvalue-problem"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.core-idea"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "Experts generally treat Kernel PCA as a clean bridge between classical PCA and kernel methods. Its long-term legacy is showing how nonlinear representation learning can be expressed through similarity matrices before deep embeddings became common.",
            "sourceIds": ["source-nonlinear-component-analysis-as-a-kernel-eigenvalue-problem"],
            "claimIds": ["claim-legacy-achievement-summary", "claim-legacy-description"],
            "contentOrigin": "commentarySections.long-term-legacy"
        },
        {
            "role": "N",
            "text": "As today's systems grow larger, which lessons from Kernel PCA still matter, and which limits deserve to be questioned again?",
